
💰 Revenue Metrics
ROAS — Return On Ad Spend
Formula  →  Revenue ÷ Ad Spend
Example  →  $3,000 ÷ $1,000 = 3.0

Meaning:
For every $1 spent on ads
you made $3 back

Below 1.0  →  Losing money ❌
1.0 – 2.0  →  Breaking even ⚠️
3.0+       →  Profitable ✅
Industry benchmark: 4.0
AOV — Average Order Value
Formula  →  Total Revenue ÷ Total Orders
Example  →  $5,000 ÷ 100 orders = $50

Meaning:
The average amount a customer
spends in a single purchase

Low AOV  →  Customers buying cheap
             items — hard to profit
High AOV →  Customers spending more
             easier to cover ad costs

How to improve:
→ Bundle products together
→ Offer upsells at checkout
→ Minimum order for free shipping
LTV — Lifetime Value
Formula  →  AOV × Purchase Frequency
             × Customer Lifespan

Example  →  $50 × 4 times/year
             × 2 years = $400

Meaning:
Total revenue one customer
brings over their entire
relationship with the business

Why it matters:
If LTV is $400 then spending
$50 to acquire that customer
is completely worth it 💪
MRR — Monthly Recurring Revenue
Formula  →  Total active subscribers
             × Monthly price

Example  →  100 customers × $29/month
             = $2,900 MRR

Meaning:
Predictable revenue coming
in every single month
Common in subscription businesses
ARR — Annual Recurring Revenue
Formula  →  MRR × 12
Example  →  $2,900 × 12 = $34,800

Meaning:
Yearly version of MRR
Used in investor reports
and business valuations
👥 Customer Metrics
CAC — Customer Acquisition Cost
Formula  →  Total Ad Spend
             ÷ New Customers Acquired

Example  →  $1,000 ÷ 20 customers
             = $50 per customer

Meaning:
How much it costs to
get ONE new customer

The golden rule:
CAC must be significantly
lower than LTV

CAC $50 + LTV $400 = ✅ Healthy
CAC $300 + LTV $50 = ❌ Dying
CVR — Conversion Rate
Formula  →  Conversions ÷ Visitors
             × 100

Example  →  50 buyers ÷ 1,000
             visitors × 100 = 5%

Meaning:
Percentage of visitors
who took the desired action
(purchased, signed up etc)

Industry benchmarks:
Ecommerce average →  2–4%
Below 1%          →  Something broken
Above 5%          →  Excellent
Churn Rate
Formula  →  Customers lost
             ÷ Total customers
             × 100

Example  →  10 lost ÷ 100 total
             × 100 = 10% churn

Meaning:
Percentage of customers
who stopped buying
or cancelled subscription

Low churn  →  Customers happy ✅
High churn →  Product or service
               problem ❌
Retention Rate
Formula  →  100% - Churn Rate

Example  →  100% - 10% = 90%

Meaning:
Percentage of customers
who stayed and came back

Opposite of churn
Higher is always better 💪
NPS — Net Promoter Score
Survey question:
"How likely are you to recommend
us to a friend? (0–10)"

Promoters  →  Score 9–10
Passives   →  Score 7–8
Detractors →  Score 0–6

Formula:
% Promoters - % Detractors

Above 50  →  Excellent ✅
0 to 50   →  Good ⚠️
Below 0   →  Crisis ❌
📊 Traffic and Campaign Metrics
CTR — Click Through Rate
Formula  →  Clicks ÷ Impressions
             × 100

Example  →  50 clicks ÷ 1,000
             impressions = 5%

Meaning:
Percentage of people who
SAW your ad and CLICKED it

Low CTR  →  Ad creative is weak
             Wrong audience targeted
High CTR →  Ad is resonating
             Audience is right
CPC — Cost Per Click
Formula  →  Total Ad Spend ÷ Clicks

Example  →  $500 ÷ 250 clicks = $2

Meaning:
How much you pay every
time someone clicks your ad

Lower CPC = more traffic
for same budget
CPM — Cost Per Mille
Formula  →  Ad Spend ÷ Impressions
             × 1,000

Example  →  $500 ÷ 50,000
             impressions × 1,000 = $10

Meaning:
How much it costs to show
your ad 1,000 times

Used for brand awareness
campaigns not sales
Impressions
Meaning:
Total number of times
your ad was SHOWN
whether clicked or not

High impressions + low CTR
→ People are seeing ad
   but not interested
CPL — Cost Per Lead
Formula  →  Ad Spend ÷ Leads Generated

Example  →  $1,000 ÷ 50 leads = $20

Meaning:
How much it costs to get
one person interested enough
to give you their contact info
📈 Performance and Growth Metrics
MoM — Month Over Month Growth
Formula  →  (This month - Last month)
             ÷ Last month × 100

Example  →  ($5,000 - $4,000)
             ÷ $4,000 × 100 = 25%

Meaning:
How much something grew
or shrank vs last month

Positive = Growing ✅
Negative = Shrinking ❌
YoY — Year Over Year Growth
Formula  →  (This year - Last year)
             ÷ Last year × 100

Meaning:
Same as MoM but compares
same month or period
across two different years

Removes seasonality effect
More reliable than MoM
for strategic decisions
GMV — Gross Merchandise Value
Meaning:
Total value of all products
sold through the platform
before any fees or returns

Used by marketplaces like
Jumia, Amazon, Shopify stores

Revenue ≠ GMV
GMV is bigger — revenue
is what the company keeps
Bounce Rate
Meaning:
Percentage of visitors who
landed on a page and LEFT
immediately without clicking
anything else

High bounce rate →  Landing page
                     is not matching
                     what the ad promised
Low bounce rate  →  Page is engaging
                     visitors are exploring
🎯 Business Goal Metrics
OKR — Objective and Key Result
Objective  →  WHERE you want to go
               "Become the #1 analytics
                tool in West Africa"

Key Result →  HOW you measure it
               "Grow revenue 40%"
               "Hit 500 new customers"
               "Achieve NPS of 60"

Review:     →  Every quarter
KPI — Key Performance Indicator
Meaning:
The specific metrics you
track to measure business
health on an ongoing basis

Different from OKRs:
KPI   →  Always tracking health
OKR   →  Time bound stretch goal

Examples of marketing KPIs:
Monthly revenue
Customer churn rate
Website conversion rate
Email open rate
ROAS per campaign
ROI — Return On Investment
Formula  →  (Revenue - Cost)
             ÷ Cost × 100

Example  →  ($3,000 - $1,000)
             ÷ $1,000 × 100 = 200%

Meaning:
Overall profitability of
any investment not just ads

ROAS is specifically for ads
ROI is for any investment
📧 Email Marketing Metrics
Open Rate
Formula  →  Emails opened
             ÷ Emails delivered × 100

Benchmark → 20–25% is healthy
Below 15% → Subject lines are weak
             or wrong audience
Click Rate
Formula  →  Email clicks
             ÷ Emails delivered × 100

Meaning:
People who opened AND
clicked something inside

Benchmark → 2–5% is healthy
Unsubscribe Rate
Benchmark → Below 0.5% is healthy
Above 1%  → Content not relevant
              or emailing too often
🗂️ The Metrics Cheat Sheet
METRIC   FORMULA                    TELLS YOU
──────────────────────────────────────────────
ROAS     Revenue ÷ Ad Spend         Campaign profit
AOV      Revenue ÷ Orders           Avg spend/order
CAC      Spend ÷ New Customers      Cost per customer
LTV      AOV × Freq × Lifespan      Customer worth
CVR      Buyers ÷ Visitors × 100    % who purchased
CTR      Clicks ÷ Impressions × 100 Ad appeal
CPC      Spend ÷ Clicks             Cost per click
CPM      Spend ÷ Impressions × 1000 Cost per 1000 views
MoM      (Now-Before)÷Before × 100  Monthly growth
YoY      (Now-Before)÷Before × 100  Yearly growth
Churn    Lost ÷ Total × 100         Customer loss rate
NPS      % Promoters - % Detractors Customer happiness
ROI      (Revenue-Cost)÷Cost × 100  Overall profit
How to Use This in Your Work
Client asks:             You immediately know:
─────────────────────────────────────────────
"Is our campaign       → Calculate ROAS
working?"                Compare to 3.0 benchmark

"Why are sales low?"   → Check CVR, CTR, Bounce
                          Find the broken step

"Are we growing?"      → Calculate MoM or YoY
                          Show the trend

"Is our pricing        → Check AOV vs CAC
right?"                   LTV must exceed CAC

"Are customers         → Calculate Churn
happy?"                   and Retention rate
